00:11:09.780 --> 00:11:14.090
for the labor of
scanning in the documents

00:11:14.090 --> 00:11:17.480
to be able to
complete this FOIA.

00:11:17.480 --> 00:11:23.960
That is a very real example
of the cost for access

00:11:23.960 --> 00:11:26.420
to this information
and the opportunity

00:11:26.420 --> 00:11:32.450
we have bringing NLP
into marketplace.

00:11:32.450 --> 00:11:35.460
I didn't get anything that
was that sophisticated,

00:11:35.460 --> 00:11:38.990
but I knew we wanted to
play in this space already.

00:11:38.990 --> 00:11:42.320
We were ready and the
technology is already available

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:45.200
that myself, as not
an expert developer,

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:48.430
can put us on the right
path for considerations

00:11:48.430 --> 00:11:53.970
of how to intelligently bring
this technology to market.

00:11:53.970 --> 00:11:58.460
The second example
is another extreme

00:11:58.460 --> 00:12:05.840
where you have to consider the
full severity of the work you

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:07.190
can do.

00:12:07.190 --> 00:12:09.150
As developers and
data scientists,

00:12:09.150 --> 00:12:10.970
the models you make--

00:12:10.970 --> 00:12:13.970
if you want to bring
something to production,

00:12:13.970 --> 00:12:16.920
it can't be racist.

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:20.220
This letter is 42
organizations that

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:25.830
penned to Axon, which is a
provider of law enforcement

00:12:25.830 --> 00:12:27.150
equipment.

00:12:27.150 --> 00:12:29.700
They were looking at
bringing to market

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:33.120
or beginning research after
putting together an AI ethics

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:39.060
board, into facial
recognition technologies.

00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:43.890
The people who would be most
affected by these technologies

00:12:43.890 --> 00:12:50.040
you work on have to be a part of
the process from the very start

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:54.810
of the development,
consideration of the idea,

00:12:54.810 --> 00:13:00.290
through the working
models, final sign-off.

00:13:00.290 --> 00:13:03.230
This-- I believe--

00:13:03.230 --> 00:13:06.650
is one of the only ways we're
going to successfully get,

00:13:06.650 --> 00:13:09.410
as a collective,
to a place where

00:13:09.410 --> 00:13:12.740
we feel very confident and safe
with the models we're building.

00:13:15.550 --> 00:13:19.480
More practically, think about
what your day-to-day work

00:13:19.480 --> 00:13:21.910
life is like.

00:13:21.910 --> 00:13:25.300
When you're thinking of
the two options of where

00:13:25.300 --> 00:13:31.701
you can go with data science
and building models for AI,

00:13:31.701 --> 00:13:33.790
I'd encourage you to
check out, obviously,

00:13:33.790 --> 00:13:36.520
the other sessions that
are deep in this space,

00:13:36.520 --> 00:13:39.700
but you see the work
that you can be doing.

00:13:39.700 --> 00:13:41.590
The experimentation,
the testing,

00:13:41.590 --> 00:13:45.110
the iterations on this model.

00:13:45.110 --> 00:13:47.600
We want to be in
this middle bucket.

00:13:47.600 --> 00:13:51.320
We want to be working
as a group all together.

00:13:51.320 --> 00:13:53.270
Experimenting.

00:13:53.270 --> 00:13:55.580
Writing scientific papers.

00:13:55.580 --> 00:13:59.690
Analyzing it and feeling
comfortable with the security

00:13:59.690 --> 00:14:00.290
we've brought.

00:14:00.290 --> 00:14:03.389
Of course, there is still
closed source development.

00:14:03.389 --> 00:14:05.180
I'm not saying that
your organization's not

00:14:05.180 --> 00:14:09.860
going to have a
proprietary take on a model

00:14:09.860 --> 00:14:11.540
that Google has made.

00:14:11.540 --> 00:14:14.300
But I think it's
increasingly unlikely

00:14:14.300 --> 00:14:18.020
that your internal
organization is

00:14:18.020 --> 00:14:22.110
going to be trying to build
the Natural Language API.

00:14:22.110 --> 00:14:25.140
You want to build the
application that uses it.

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:29.580
You want to be able to train
your own models for something

00:14:29.580 --> 00:14:31.510
like that.

00:14:31.510 --> 00:14:36.450
But we're not going to want
to be in this left bucket,

00:14:36.450 --> 00:14:40.110
where you, as a developer or
a data scientist, your team--

00:14:40.110 --> 00:14:43.890
no matter how well-constructed,
well-developed--

00:14:43.890 --> 00:14:47.160
you don't want to be
standing out isolated.

00:14:47.160 --> 00:14:52.380
You don't want to be bringing
to market production models that

00:14:52.380 --> 00:14:55.680
haven't been proved by
the community at large.

00:14:55.680 --> 00:14:58.710
We want a stringent
testing system.

00:14:58.710 --> 00:15:03.510
And these questions
weighed really heavy on us

00:15:03.510 --> 00:15:06.810
when we are looking
at NLP, even for just

00:15:06.810 --> 00:15:12.910
analyzing our own writing
and regulatory work.

00:15:12.910 --> 00:15:18.880
So here's the question.

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:20.940
You've got these two options.

00:15:20.940 --> 00:15:26.340
I would love out of this group--
because I've seen the job

00:15:26.340 --> 00:15:30.690
titles and organizations
who are represented here--

00:15:30.690 --> 00:15:32.940
if I could get a
quick, close your eyes

00:15:32.940 --> 00:15:35.430
and raise your hands for
either the first or second.

00:15:35.430 --> 00:15:38.730
I would be fascinated to
know for my own knowledge.

00:15:38.730 --> 00:15:41.970
So quick eyes closed,
raise your hand

00:15:41.970 --> 00:15:44.474
if our first option
is, we're not

00:15:44.474 --> 00:15:46.140
going to be able to
get there right now.

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:48.210
You're not going to be
able to hit production

00:15:48.210 --> 00:15:53.490
because the regulation, the
technical obstacles-- it's not

00:15:53.490 --> 00:15:54.330
clear enough.

00:15:54.330 --> 00:15:58.080
We are not in a safe space
to be able to launch.

00:15:58.080 --> 00:16:05.790
Or we're in the bottom
question where right now, you

00:16:05.790 --> 00:16:08.460
could go home after
this conference

00:16:08.460 --> 00:16:12.180
and start a project on Monday,
work with stakeholders,

00:16:12.180 --> 00:16:15.360
diligently build a new
tool that you could bring

00:16:15.360 --> 00:16:18.390
to market in your company
by the end of the year that

00:16:18.390 --> 00:16:23.640
would safely have in
consideration the second.

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:27.090
And I apologize, there was one
person who closed their eyes

00:16:27.090 --> 00:16:29.070
and raised their hand.

00:16:29.070 --> 00:16:30.780
Now we can ask the question.

00:16:30.780 --> 00:16:32.220
Close your eyes.

00:16:32.220 --> 00:16:33.570
Raise your hand for one.

00:16:36.310 --> 00:16:39.290
And second.

00:16:39.290 --> 00:16:41.750
Wonderful, that's
really exciting to see.

00:16:41.750 --> 00:16:45.530
It's exciting to see that
probably, 70% of the room

00:16:45.530 --> 00:16:49.550
was enthusiastic about
our second option.

00:16:49.550 --> 00:16:53.690
Truth is, I feel
like whichever you

00:16:53.690 --> 00:16:56.660
pick is going to be
the route you go.

00:16:56.660 --> 00:16:59.250
This slide on the right--

00:16:59.250 --> 00:17:00.980
some you might recognize it--

00:17:00.980 --> 00:17:03.710
this is produced
from our US Senate.

00:17:03.710 --> 00:17:07.700
It's an analysis of
the solutions we could

00:17:07.700 --> 00:17:10.670
take to prevent a Y2K crisis.

00:17:10.670 --> 00:17:15.410
What I love about this is on
the left hand on your y-axis,

00:17:15.410 --> 00:17:18.950
that's month to implement.

00:17:18.950 --> 00:17:22.190
[? Day-to-day ?] expansions
is going to take us 48 months

00:17:22.190 --> 00:17:26.510
to get there, but it gives the
highest performance reliability

00:17:26.510 --> 00:17:29.420
standards against a Y2K bug.

00:17:29.420 --> 00:17:32.630
TensorFlow came out last year.

00:17:32.630 --> 00:17:37.930
You think of the context around
the development of our space.

00:17:37.930 --> 00:17:39.740
And it's not just
software development.

00:17:39.740 --> 00:17:41.270
It's the architecture.

00:17:41.270 --> 00:17:44.690
It's the hardware that you run
your models and the networkings

00:17:44.690 --> 00:17:47.320
that you're running it on.

00:17:47.320 --> 00:17:50.350
This seems irrelevant,
but we will equally

00:17:50.350 --> 00:17:56.300
have similar lack of
understanding when we look back

00:17:56.300 --> 00:17:59.210
on our current
analysis of AI today,

00:17:59.210 --> 00:18:03.060
where we really missed
the mark on some things,

00:18:03.060 --> 00:18:06.890
but I do like just
rely on the experts.

00:18:06.890 --> 00:18:09.070
I'm not going to be making
any of this up myself.

00:18:09.070 --> 00:18:11.540
I'm not a leader
in machine learning

00:18:11.540 --> 00:18:13.070
and artificial intelligence.

00:18:13.070 --> 00:18:14.090
I'm a user.

00:18:14.090 --> 00:18:17.630
I'm someone who practically
has day-to-day tasks I want

00:18:17.630 --> 00:18:22.280
to get done and I am
grateful to be in a space

00:18:22.280 --> 00:18:27.260
where leaders like
Fei-Fei Li are carving out

00:18:27.260 --> 00:18:31.590
this space for us, for us
to be able to do our work.

00:18:31.590 --> 00:18:35.750
So from 2017,
during her session,

00:18:35.750 --> 00:18:37.580
these were four
principal components

00:18:37.580 --> 00:18:43.490
for democratizing AI
that are essential for--

00:18:43.490 --> 00:18:48.520
if we go back to
stakeholder involvement,

00:18:48.520 --> 00:18:52.610
you look at the Axon example
and this is a perfect chance

00:18:52.610 --> 00:18:55.280
to consider whether or
not we're really looking

00:18:55.280 --> 00:18:58.790
at a democratized AI scenario.

00:18:58.790 --> 00:19:01.400
Making sure everybody
who would be impacted

00:19:01.400 --> 00:19:04.550
by an artificial
intelligence solution

00:19:04.550 --> 00:19:08.690
has access to computing
resources to test it.

00:19:08.690 --> 00:19:12.140
Has access to
training and resources

00:19:12.140 --> 00:19:15.170
so that they themselves
can become the talent that

00:19:15.170 --> 00:19:18.470
works on these projects.

00:19:18.470 --> 00:19:20.870
Making sure the
algorithms and the data

00:19:20.870 --> 00:19:23.300
that you're using and
bringing to market

00:19:23.300 --> 00:19:29.970
is accessible to a scientific
and open community,

00:19:29.970 --> 00:19:34.960
is going to make this
slide a lot more fun.

00:19:34.960 --> 00:19:36.880
I've been playing in
the bottom left hand

00:19:36.880 --> 00:19:44.020
corner, where our budgets will
range from tens of dollars.

00:19:44.020 --> 00:19:47.740
I look at if I can
spend less than $100

00:19:47.740 --> 00:19:50.440
and deliver you some
sort of business value,

00:19:50.440 --> 00:19:55.510
I only have to save someone 10
minutes to make back that $100.

00:19:55.510 --> 00:20:00.320
So I look at big opportunities
to carve out time savings.

00:20:00.320 --> 00:20:01.960
And that's prototyping tools.

00:20:01.960 --> 00:20:04.330
It's not going to be something
that can live forever.

00:20:04.330 --> 00:20:05.860
It's going to have
instabilities.

00:20:05.860 --> 00:20:09.760
There are clear spots where
that wasn't built out because it

00:20:09.760 --> 00:20:11.500
wasn't supposed to be.

00:20:11.500 --> 00:20:13.240
We don't want to
spend our developers

00:20:13.240 --> 00:20:17.890
time working on something that's
not going to come to market.

00:20:17.890 --> 00:20:19.780
And practically, my
organization doesn't

00:20:19.780 --> 00:20:23.740
have a need for an enterprise
natural language processing

00:20:23.740 --> 00:20:24.850
tool.

00:20:24.850 --> 00:20:28.830
We got a question we want to
answer and AI helped us do it.

00:20:28.830 --> 00:20:32.730
This top right corner,
though, I think we're all

00:20:32.730 --> 00:20:35.130
passionate about getting there.

00:20:35.130 --> 00:20:41.535
The potential impact of
bringing to market an IoT health

00:20:41.535 --> 00:20:47.790
care device that is able
to consistently monitor

00:20:47.790 --> 00:20:52.530
your glucose levels and it works
even when the power goes off

00:20:52.530 --> 00:20:54.330
and the internet's gone.

00:20:54.330 --> 00:20:57.750
And it's able to help
predict critical failures

00:20:57.750 --> 00:20:59.160
of your health.

00:20:59.160 --> 00:21:02.820
We all know we want to
be making new markets

00:21:02.820 --> 00:21:10.680
and changing people's lives with
AI, but the risks around that

00:21:10.680 --> 00:21:12.420
are scary.

00:21:12.420 --> 00:21:15.840
I would say, for developers,
we've got some concerns.

00:21:15.840 --> 00:21:19.620
But primarily, for investors.

00:21:19.620 --> 00:21:22.230
There's maybe,
$10 billion a year

00:21:22.230 --> 00:21:24.960
in the US pouring
into AI research.

00:21:24.960 --> 00:21:28.770
And I think you
could short that.

00:21:28.770 --> 00:21:34.170
I think you could safely say,
10X-ing that AI research would

00:21:34.170 --> 00:21:38.040
still be a fair safe investment.

00:21:38.040 --> 00:21:40.020
You clearly haven't
seen it, though,

00:21:40.020 --> 00:21:43.680
and until some of those
regulatory legal concerns

00:21:43.680 --> 00:21:46.680
get worked through and resolved
and they're a safe space

00:21:46.680 --> 00:21:50.910
to play and bring
to market, then

00:21:50.910 --> 00:21:53.700
maybe we continue working
on prototyping tools

00:21:53.700 --> 00:21:56.010
and then bring them
to internal tools.

00:21:56.010 --> 00:21:59.460
And continue to
develop a culture that

00:21:59.460 --> 00:22:02.280
knows how to use
AI applications,

00:22:02.280 --> 00:22:06.900
wants to build these things,
and has tested it rigorously.

00:22:06.900 --> 00:22:09.270
You don't want in
your first one to be

00:22:09.270 --> 00:22:11.860
the one that goes to market.

00:22:11.860 --> 00:22:15.300
So there's a lot on this
lot and I apologize.

00:22:15.300 --> 00:22:18.390
Let's start with the most
interesting information.

00:22:18.390 --> 00:22:19.920
This is a paper--

00:22:19.920 --> 00:22:22.640
I believe this is
a Google team--

00:22:22.640 --> 00:22:25.650
that they presented
initially in February

00:22:25.650 --> 00:22:28.980
and it won a bunch
of awards in June.

00:22:28.980 --> 00:22:31.080
Obfuscated gradients
give a false sense

00:22:31.080 --> 00:22:33.360
of security
circumventing defenses

00:22:33.360 --> 00:22:36.050
to adversarial examples.

00:22:36.050 --> 00:22:39.590
That's frightening-- I feel.

00:22:39.590 --> 00:22:44.060
At the conference, they
won best presentation.

00:22:44.060 --> 00:22:51.500
There were eight proclaimed
secure white box algorithms

00:22:51.500 --> 00:22:55.250
and this took them all down.

00:22:55.250 --> 00:22:58.200
This came out in March.

00:22:58.200 --> 00:23:03.020
TensorFlow 1.0, which
most data scientists--

00:23:03.020 --> 00:23:05.744
not that there's not
other models like that.

00:23:05.744 --> 00:23:07.160
It's not like you
can't use Keras.

00:23:07.160 --> 00:23:10.574
It's not like there's
H20.AI, but TensorFlow's

00:23:10.574 --> 00:23:11.490
a really good example.

00:23:11.490 --> 00:23:16.190
You look at the forks,
there's clearly interest.

00:23:16.190 --> 00:23:18.800
That's not enough time in
between these two events

00:23:18.800 --> 00:23:23.060
happening for me to
feel confident yet.

00:23:23.060 --> 00:23:25.940
The other side of
this is the context,

00:23:25.940 --> 00:23:30.530
is the thing that's weighing
us down in this consideration.

00:23:30.530 --> 00:23:34.934
We've got Tim
Berners-Lee in 1989.

00:23:34.934 --> 00:23:37.100
I don't think it makes sense
to start a conversation

00:23:37.100 --> 00:23:40.730
about open web before that.

00:23:40.730 --> 00:23:45.710
And then we have the cost
estimates for that Y2K bug.

00:23:45.710 --> 00:23:49.730
It looks like Aetna
might be at $195 million

00:23:49.730 --> 00:23:51.860
if Y2K doesn't go well.

00:23:51.860 --> 00:23:57.780
And those figures seem extreme
and they seem goofy now,

00:23:57.780 --> 00:23:59.210
but they didn't at the time.

00:23:59.210 --> 00:24:04.520
I feel like we are going
to have similarly goofy--

00:24:04.520 --> 00:24:09.890
not goofy, but misunderstandings
about where we are in the cycle

00:24:09.890 --> 00:24:12.740
as we see in 1999.

00:24:12.740 --> 00:24:16.370
And this last component
just elaborates on the idea

00:24:16.370 --> 00:24:18.110
of investing in tools.

00:24:18.110 --> 00:24:21.716
The left framework, I would
highly encourage you--

00:24:21.716 --> 00:24:23.090
honestly, if
there's anything you

00:24:23.090 --> 00:24:26.270
took away out of this
development talk,

00:24:26.270 --> 00:24:27.470
it would be that framework.

00:24:27.470 --> 00:24:31.280
It wasn't even originally
intended for developers.

00:24:31.280 --> 00:24:35.720
It's the framework for strategic
sustainable development.

00:24:35.720 --> 00:24:40.010
Came out of a group in
Sweden in the early '90s.

00:24:40.010 --> 00:24:43.670
It's about sustainability
in the world

00:24:43.670 --> 00:24:47.420
and it really drills it
down into a systems level.

00:24:47.420 --> 00:24:52.700
If we talk about the system of
AI development and health care,

00:24:52.700 --> 00:24:54.740
that would include
all of the algorithms.

00:24:54.740 --> 00:24:55.760
The talent pool.

00:24:55.760 --> 00:24:58.310
The hardware that's available
for you to run it in.

00:24:58.310 --> 00:24:59.570
The end devices.

00:24:59.570 --> 00:25:02.000
The systems that
subscribe to it.

00:25:02.000 --> 00:25:05.510
The entire ecosystem of
everything that you touch.

00:25:05.510 --> 00:25:07.670
It's very hard to change that.

00:25:07.670 --> 00:25:12.350
And it's a collective effort
to make changes to the system.

00:25:12.350 --> 00:25:14.900
But making tools
is easier and tools

00:25:14.900 --> 00:25:19.730
are what we use to take
action in our day-to-day jobs.

00:25:19.730 --> 00:25:23.180
If we've got better tools
to do the work that we want

00:25:23.180 --> 00:25:26.570
to accomplish, there's
tangible benefits

00:25:26.570 --> 00:25:31.130
in our ability to execute
the organization's strategy.

00:25:31.130 --> 00:25:34.340
Do your job better, do your
job easier thanks to the tools.

00:25:34.340 --> 00:25:37.010
Now you can execute
your strategy.

00:25:37.010 --> 00:25:41.750
And if we align our strategy
with some smart goals,

00:25:41.750 --> 00:25:44.600
that feeds all the way
up to a great system.

00:25:44.600 --> 00:25:47.180
But the main thing
that I can say

00:25:47.180 --> 00:25:49.520
that we're focusing
on right now is

00:25:49.520 --> 00:25:53.720
making sure we've got talent
who we feel comfortable working

00:25:53.720 --> 00:26:00.530
with and we've got a similar
dialogue around these tools.

00:26:00.530 --> 00:26:03.470
We want to test a lot of
platforms and services.

00:26:03.470 --> 00:26:05.480
I'm here at Google's
presentation,

00:26:05.480 --> 00:26:09.140
but I would love to
run similar experiments

00:26:09.140 --> 00:26:12.350
across the enterprise
solutions here.

00:26:12.350 --> 00:26:14.420
There is a certain
amount of you don't

00:26:14.420 --> 00:26:16.490
want to factor your
efforts too much

00:26:16.490 --> 00:26:20.090
and I've been very happy in
the ecosystem I operate in.

00:26:20.090 --> 00:26:23.180
I do all of our work in
Google Cloud Platform.

00:26:23.180 --> 00:26:23.790
But

00:26:23.790 --> 00:26:25.670
We want to evaluate
the platforms.

00:26:25.670 --> 00:26:28.670
And your products should
still deliver business value

00:26:28.670 --> 00:26:31.910
if your manager comes
up and asks hey,

00:26:31.910 --> 00:26:33.290
I see these API calls.

00:26:33.290 --> 00:26:35.060
What is this?

00:26:35.060 --> 00:26:39.380
There should be something that
you're trying to get out of it.

00:26:39.380 --> 00:26:41.900
One of the additional
components of framework

00:26:41.900 --> 00:26:47.270
for strategic sustainable
development is backcasting.

00:26:47.270 --> 00:26:50.360
Understanding where
you want to arrive

00:26:50.360 --> 00:26:55.340
and where you are today and
understanding what steps

00:26:55.340 --> 00:26:59.990
you would be taking to get
to that eventual end goal.

00:26:59.990 --> 00:27:03.710
As opposed to forecasting,
saying historically,

00:27:03.710 --> 00:27:05.270
here's what we've done.

00:27:05.270 --> 00:27:09.980
Based off of those projections,
we project we will get here.

00:27:09.980 --> 00:27:10.890
This is the opposite.

00:27:10.890 --> 00:27:14.680
We know if we want
to get to market,

00:27:14.680 --> 00:27:17.840
we are going to need a great
development team that's fully

00:27:17.840 --> 00:27:21.470
bought in and is well-staffed.

00:27:21.470 --> 00:27:24.440
We need stakeholder
buy-in from the community.

00:27:24.440 --> 00:27:26.660
You need the patients
who are going

00:27:26.660 --> 00:27:30.781
to be affected by your product
to know about it from the

00:27:30.781 --> 00:27:31.280
get go.

00:27:31.280 --> 00:27:33.860
From the first time
you had the idea

00:27:33.860 --> 00:27:41.750
to use AI to better analyze
rhythmic patterns of the heart.

00:27:41.750 --> 00:27:44.180
You should probably talk
to some heart patients

00:27:44.180 --> 00:27:48.560
and find out about
their experiences.

00:27:48.560 --> 00:27:51.260
Having regulations
in place would help.

00:27:51.260 --> 00:27:54.021
Having very clear
guidance in that space

00:27:54.021 --> 00:27:55.520
and that's going
to be a combination

00:27:55.520 --> 00:27:58.520
from the government
getting involved,

00:27:58.520 --> 00:28:04.760
as well as from the tech side
being leaders and advocates.

00:28:04.760 --> 00:28:06.650
Cloud-first
architecture for data--

00:28:06.650 --> 00:28:07.720
I think that makes sense.

00:28:07.720 --> 00:28:09.950
We're at Google NEXT.

00:28:09.950 --> 00:28:15.110
And the ability to run on-device
and in cloud computation

00:28:15.110 --> 00:28:17.930
for the results, I
do think mentioning

00:28:17.930 --> 00:28:21.290
on-device calculations
is important.

00:28:21.290 --> 00:28:26.180
One of the really interesting
examples is TensorFlow.js,

00:28:26.180 --> 00:28:30.140
but I think fundamentally
being able to do

00:28:30.140 --> 00:28:33.990
localized calculations
is extremely helpful.

00:28:33.990 --> 00:28:37.130
So I want to pivot now.

00:28:37.130 --> 00:28:39.590
This is the switch
over to practical--

00:28:39.590 --> 00:28:42.240
here's what the work looks like.

00:28:42.240 --> 00:28:45.620
So how to analyze
hundreds of pages.

00:28:45.620 --> 00:28:50.550
I'll give you a couple of slides
on the development process

00:28:50.550 --> 00:28:53.730
and then, we'll switch
over to the live demo.

00:28:53.730 --> 00:28:56.900
This is just a
consideration pyramid

00:28:56.900 --> 00:29:00.570
for when you are taking
on a project like this.

00:29:00.570 --> 00:29:03.200
Who are you going to
be presenting this to?

00:29:03.200 --> 00:29:05.750
If you are sharing
the work you're

00:29:05.750 --> 00:29:08.510
doing with this with
a development team

00:29:08.510 --> 00:29:09.570
lead-- for example.

00:29:09.570 --> 00:29:13.040
Someone who's very interested
in the hows and whys this

00:29:13.040 --> 00:29:17.000
works, but wouldn't be
concerned about the appearances.

00:29:17.000 --> 00:29:20.180
Maybe you don't need to
worry about throwing up

00:29:20.180 --> 00:29:22.670
during the prototype
development--

00:29:22.670 --> 00:29:25.370
a firewall that
secures all of this

00:29:25.370 --> 00:29:26.990
within your own
internal network.

00:29:26.990 --> 00:29:31.040
Or you don't need HTTPS
access and it's just

00:29:31.040 --> 00:29:34.820
going to show up as a
nice beautiful IP address

00:29:34.820 --> 00:29:39.620
that they know you could deploy
to a front end if you wanted.

00:29:39.620 --> 00:29:43.640
On the other hand, if you're
going to be taking this work

00:29:43.640 --> 00:29:46.400
and you're going to spend
four or five hours building

00:29:46.400 --> 00:29:50.335
something that you feel really
comfortable and confident in

00:29:50.335 --> 00:29:51.710
and you're going
to be sharing it

00:29:51.710 --> 00:29:58.790
with senior executives in the
finance side who really don't

00:29:58.790 --> 00:30:02.510
have an interest in the hows
and the whys of launching

00:30:02.510 --> 00:30:07.370
a server in Compute Engine
and why that might not be--

00:30:07.370 --> 00:30:11.240
it's not an unnecessary
expense or time sync,

00:30:11.240 --> 00:30:12.950
but it looks pretty.

00:30:12.950 --> 00:30:17.660
Having the green check mark
in a nice formatted URL

00:30:17.660 --> 00:30:20.420
does help get the
point across clearer.

00:30:20.420 --> 00:30:23.150
And if that's all
the amount of lift

00:30:23.150 --> 00:30:27.140
you need to do to go from
they don't understand it

00:30:27.140 --> 00:30:31.700
at all to the idea clicks, I
see where this would help us

00:30:31.700 --> 00:30:33.860
and we want love
to do more of this.

00:30:33.860 --> 00:30:36.320
Or even better, they
see it and they've

00:30:36.320 --> 00:30:39.860
got their own ideas of I
would love something like this

00:30:39.860 --> 00:30:41.690
